"""The Necrobinder's strategy on game build v0.111, shared by planning and individual decisions.

Owner's guidance: card removals and a smaller deck, Block and preserving Osty's HP, while
looking for scaling.

The numbers in the text are what the highest-rated Spire Codex players did with the Necrobinder on
this build: 537 solo A10 standard runs of builds v0.111.1 and v0.111.0 by the 20 highest-rated accounts
that have them, 371 won, read 2026-11-25 with ``python -m sts_agent.pilot --build v0.111.0 players
--character NECROBINDER --players 20 --runs 40 --words Osty,Summon,Doom,Soul,Ethereal`` (the decks
carried into each act boss and what was taken at 9634 card rewards, counted by
``evidence.deck_balance``'s role rules). The simulated numbers are ``pilot.runs`` on Spire Mentor's
v0.111.0 simulator: 120 A10 runs drafted by its run-policy net, fights searched, no model and no live
run. The build names come from the Spire Codex guides (builds guide, Necrobinder tier list, "Offensive
Necrobinder" and "Necrobinder's True Potential").
"""

PROMPT_VERSION = "necrobinder-strategic-planner-prompt-v0111-2"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE NECROBINDER'S DECK. The deck stays smaller than the other characters' by removing cards and by refusing attacks, not by refusing everything. In 537 solo A10 runs on this game version by the highest-rated Spire Codex players (371 won), the deck entering the act 1, 2 and 3 bosses held about 19, 25 and 31 cards after four removals a run, the most of any character, and they took 85% of the card rewards in act 1, 68% in act 2 and 54% in act 3. They followed no size limit: with 24 or more cards in act 2 they took seven rewards in ten, a little more often than with fewer. Removals come before additions from act 2 on, where those players made half of theirs (two a run): at a shop buy the removal before any card. In act 1 the shop's gold first goes to what wins the act 1 fights, and a removal is bought with what is left. Prefer events and relics that remove or transform; remove starter Strikes first (four in five of those players' act 1 removals, under half later, when an added card that no longer earns its draw goes as often), then curses, and a Defend only late, because this deck's damage comes from Osty, Doom and a few multiplied attacks while its plain Block is thin.

Block and Osty's HP are one defensive budget, and Weak protects both. While Osty is alive he absorbs all unblocked attack damage, so his HP is Block that lasts between turns, and it is also his damage; an enemy that is Weak or has lost Strength takes less from both. Cards that apply Weak or lower enemy Strength were what those players took most: seven times in ten with none or one in the deck, three in four among the first three picks, and still about half the time with four. The deck_balance track "weak" asks for four, five in a deck over 28 cards; while it is short such a card comes first, ahead of plain Block, and one that also gains Block is the best of them. A Block card was taken four times in ten up to the fourth and a quarter of the time after, so Block is never marked over-supplied. A large Summon was taken more than half the time, the cheap Summon cards one time in five and less the more the deck held: the starter's Summon and one large one keep Osty up, and Summon is not added for its own sake. A card that kills Osty for a one-time effect (the track "osty") gives up the HP the Summon cards built and the attacks that use it: those players took a first one four times in ten in act 1, for the damage to ALL enemies it gives that act's fights, and a second one time in ten. One is carried; never a second, and none after act 1 without a named way to get the fight finished by it.

What those players refused is attacks: an attack that only deals damage one time in six in act 1 and one in thirteen after, and a damage card of any kind 27% of the time with none added, 15% with three and 11% with six; deck_balance marks damage over-supplied once the deck holds more damage cards than those decks did (from the fifth in act 1, the seventh in act 2, the ninth after). Doom cards are the exception once the deck is built on them: they were taken one time in seven with none in the deck, three in ten with two or three and four in ten with four or five; Soul cards, their Ethereal payoffs and Osty's attacks rise the same way, less steeply. So add them to a route that exists and not as a second route. Energy, draw and other utility were taken more than a third of the time at any count and four times in ten after act 1, the best of them two times in three or more: keep adding them. Scaling, because flat damage does not end act 2 and act 3 bosses: a damage-scaling Power (one that multiplies attack damage or what Vulnerable adds, most often) was taken 46% of the time as the first source, which is also what the first picks go to with Weak and Block, 36% as the second and a quarter after; those decks held one at the act 1 boss and two at the act 3 boss. The community guides name three engines, Souls with their Ethereal payoffs, Osty, and Doom (an enemy dies at the end of its turn once its Doom reaches its HP), of which two together win, and a small offensive build of Weak-and-Block cards with damage multipliers and cards that return a card from the discard pile to replay it. One route that the deck draws every cycle beats two half-built ones.
"""

ELITE_GUIDANCE = """\
THE NECROBINDER'S ELITE ROUTES. The Necrobinder has the lowest HP of the five and no healing after fights, so HP spent on the route is not coming back before the next rest site, and elites are where it goes. Two measurements bound the judgment. In simulated A10 runs on this game version a strong drafter lost about one elite in eight in act 1 and one in four in act 2 with this character, against one in forty and one in twenty with the Ironclad, and an elite it beat cost a third of max HP. The highest-rated players take them all the same: 2.4 elites in act 1, 2.1 in act 2 and 1.8 in act 3 a run, losing 17%, 18% and 13% of max HP to each, and they enter the act 1 boss at half HP. What their deck holds by the act 1 boss is about nine added cards on three starter Strikes: one or two cards that apply Weak, three Block cards, three or four that deal damage, one scaling piece and one or two energy or draw cards. So elites are taken selectively in every act, and act 1 can plan none when the deck, HP or recovery cannot support one. Take act 1 elites when the deck is on that pace and Osty's HP and its Block can absorb the elite's big turns: a route with a rest site before or right behind the elite, and Weak or Block picked up in the rooms before it. An elite with several enemies or summons needs an attack that hits ALL enemies or Doom on ALL enemies first. From act 2 on prefer routes that pass a shop, because the removal is then this deck's best purchase, and count it when comparing a shop route with an extra elite. In later acts plan at most one elite, behind a rest site, while Block scaling or damage scaling is still missing; a second with HP to spare and a rest site between them. State which elites are accepted, where the shop and rest sites fall, and what would remove or add one.
"""


# What one top player said aloud while playing this character at ascension 10:
# JapaneseExport, "Why pros win with bad cards (and you don't)", game v0.111.0 beta, seed AHYGF81CRD4P.
# Notes taken from the video's narration and screens. It is one run by one player, so the text gives
# reasons to weigh and no rates, and names cards by what they do, because the card on offer carries its own text.
NARRATED_RUN = """\
HOW A TOP PLAYER REASONED WITH THE NECROBINDER. One narrated A10 win on this game version: reasons to weigh beside the numbers above, not rates (its deck ended at 28 cards). The deck was built as one loop and not as separate Soul, Osty and Doom packages: a small deck that keeps drawing the starter's Summon card and the attack whose damage grows with Osty's current HP, with Soul cards, cheap draw and a card that returns cards from the discard pile all judged as ways to reach those two. Because that attack counts Osty's HP, Block that keeps Osty healthy is also damage; his HP is spent only for progress that ends the fight sooner. Retain was bought for those two cards, at the full price of the event that sold it: a held attack chooses the turn of the big hit, and a held card is not redrawn at the wrong time. An energy card rated among the character's best was taken at the first reward although it made the deck weaker until removals came, and the next choices paid for that at once: a zero-cost attack for damage now, the Summon card upgraded, a starter Strike removed (called nearly as good as adding a Block card, for what is drawn in its place) and the first elite left out while the deck lacked damage and needed its rest sites. A second copy of that energy card was refused every time, upgraded or not. Under pressure a card that hits and applies Vulnerable now was taken over one with more promise; the promising one was taken an act later, when a Power that turns attack damage into Doom was held and the opening turns were safe. Strength lost as a card's drawback cost this deck little, since Osty's attacks and Doom do not use it, and Strength scaling from another character's cards was refused for the same reason. A Power that pays whenever Doom is applied was skipped, newly strengthened or not, while the deck applied no Doom. Long and short fights got different tools: the Doom Power shortens long fights, and one attack that grows for the rest of the run each time it is played ends short ones and buys the turns the Powers need (that attack was called too strong on this build). With both held, further damage cards were skipped, but a second copy of a multiplier for the first attack of each turn was bought, because two of them make one decisive hit. A relic that protects the first turn was chosen because it lets an expensive Power be played at once, and an energy relic was bought once the deck drew more than it could play. Elites followed the deck: sought in act 2 once new relics had made it strong, left out in act 3 for the most rest sites once the run was winning and a potion that prevents death was held (that run's judgment; the players counted above still took nearly two in act 3). A curse costs this deck more than any other, because it draws every card in every pass.
"""

DECK_GUIDANCE += "\n" + NARRATED_RUN
